  "description": "A real CheapOair booking confirmation (a short one-way Air Canada domestic flight, Vancouver to Castlegar, printed once as departure header and once as arrival, both the same calendar day) returned two different, wrong dates: departureDate 2026-03-20 and returnDate 2019-03-20. Root cause: the ticket's two date lines were OCR'd with inconsistent spacing around the same physical date -- \"Wed, Mar20,2019\" (zero separator between month and day) vs \"Wed, Mar 20, 2019\" (spaced). The spaced line matched monthDayYear correctly (2019-03-20). The zero-separator line failed monthDayYear, which required at least one separator character between month and day, so it fell through to the yearless monthDayNoYear fallback and inferred a year near referenceDate instead of using the real, explicit 2019 printed right there -- producing 2026-03-20. Because the two lines then resolved to two different string values, they were treated as two distinct dates instead of being deduplicated as the same one, so the ambiguous-date-pool fallback assigned the first (wrong) one as departure and the second (correct) one as return, when this ticket is actually one-way with no return date at all. Fixed by loosening monthDayYear's month-day separator the same way dayMonthYear's day-year separator was loosened in 0.7.0 for PAL's \"01Jun2026\": both lines now resolve to the same 2019-03-20, dedup collapses them to one date, and the single-date branch of the fallback assigns it as departureDate with no returnDate -- matching this ticket's actual one-way itinerary. (Whether a departure date being in the past should be rejected is a downstream eligibility-matching concern, not something this extractor validates -- it passes through whatever date is actually printed.)",
